<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jean Louis Burckhardt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt was a Swiss traveller and orientalist who explored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> in the guise of a Muslim scholar, Sheikh Ibrahim. In 1812 he noticed inscribed stones at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>, later recognised as Hittite hieroglyphs, and became the first European in modern times to see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>. He also visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> before dying of dysentery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1817.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt.jpg" alt="Johann Ludwig Burckhardt" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Ludwig_Burckhardt.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
